{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli reigned from 1154 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusades and the history of the County of Tripoli. He was a skilled military leader a shrewd diplomat and a powerful ruler navigating a complex political landscape in the Levant. His reign witnessed both expansion and challenges to Tripoli's authority and independence.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He received extensive military training preparing him for a life of leadership. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli at the age of 24. His early rule was marked by consolidating his power and dealing with internal dissent. He faced opposition from rival factions within the city and from neighboring rulers who sought to undermine Tripoli’s position. He established a strong administrative system and worked to improve the county’s defenses.\n\nRaymond III's reign was largely defined by his military campaigns against Muslim forces. He engaged in numerous battles reclaiming lost territories and defending Tripoli's borders. He allied with other Crusader states such as the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch forming strategic partnerships to counter the expanding power of the Zengids under Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. His military prowess earned him respect among his vassals and enemies alike.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a master of diplomacy skillfully maneuvering between various powers. He maintained a delicate balance of alliances attempting to secure Tripoli’s independence and prosperity. He frequently engaged in negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to secure truces and trade agreements. He understood that military strength alone was not enough to ensure Tripoli's survival and prioritized diplomatic solutions whenever possible. He forged alliances with the Knights Templar and the Knights Hospitaller bolstering Tripoli’s defenses and economic strength. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in maintaining a degree of stability in a volatile region.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1194 after a long and eventful reign. He left behind a strengthened county with a robust military and a well-developed administrative system. He is remembered as one of the most successful and influential Counts of Tripoli. His reign solidified Tripoli’s position as a major player in the Crusader states and ensured its survival for several decades after his death. He is often lauded for his military leadership his diplomatic skills and his commitment to defending the Christian presence in the Levant. His legacy continues to be studied by historians seeking to understand the complexities of the Crusades and the political landscape of the medieval Middle East. He is considered a pivotal figure in the history of the County of Tripoli.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁reigned", "logprob": -1.7908426523208618}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.10313314199447632}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -1.597391747054644e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.0874729418428615e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -6.9141146923357155e-06}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -1.7787184715270996}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.2662537097930908}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -7.319182623177767e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "9", "logprob": -0.00010966652916977182}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.011853143572807312}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.05372386425733566}, {"token": "▁significant", "logprob": -0.03083